            <ArticleTitle>Conservative surgical management for diffuse uterine adenomyosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>715-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.fertnstert.2009.03.046</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the effects of new conservative surgical management for diffuse uterine adenomyosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective clinical study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Gynecology department in a general hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 44 women with diffuse adenomyosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Conservative surgical management.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Mean visual analog scale score of dysmenorrhea and menorrhagia after a 3-month follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After this surgery, the mean visual analog scale score of dysmenorrhea decreased from 9.4 to 0.8, and anemia due to menorrhagia improved in all women. Two patients became pregnant, with one interstitial pregnancy and one normal pregnancy that is continuing. No major complications or sequelae were observed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This procedure should be considered as a therapeutic option in women with symptoms of diffuse uterine adenomyosis who wish to avoid hysterectomy.</AbstractText>
